mer which I actually might be a  target developer partner so you can kind  of put that framework loosely in place  but then you know once you've kind of  done that from a potential standpoint  you really just have to focus on  building the best product that's most  enticing and engaging for the developer  and putting that effort into it so that  you're really realizing that potential  value that you've established  Nepos and you can't you can't plan for  innovation and and to your point earlier  this there's probably far more  innovators outside the organization than  internally so you've you found some  people doing interesting things you  weren't anticipating you know but of  course you know being a we always had a  customers being able to try get very  very close to the end-user to the sort  of business center developers and then  be able to iterate quickly on how you  support them with API products I think  that ability iterate quickly you'll find  where the value is and they can start  building on top of that so the next  question I remembered the benefit of  experience if you can go back to the  start of the Walgreens API program is  there anything that you would do  differently yeah so honestly I mean I'd  say not a lot and I think you saw in my  story that we got really lucky that we  actually kind of had a lot of the right  pieces in place and I think we did a lot  of the right things from the beginning  and I you know I understand that not  everyone might have that that good  fortune to have all the right things in  place so I would just suggest for others  well I wouldn't necessarily change to  you know for another company to try to  replicate as many of those conditions  that I talked about as you can at least  to the extent that you can control it so  and that being said I do think that you  know if there was one thing I could do a  little bit differently was maybe  engaging a little bit earlier with our  digital marketing team about our  marketing strategy I think there's some  really unique things here about how you  market API programs that you know I  think we've been still working through  which is you know you really want the  the benefit the program you want the the  appetite and the engagement for the  program but the brand that's driving it  isn't your own brand and so the  considerations are a little bit  different again I think we're getting  there but I think if we had started  earlier remove  perfected that dance a little bit better  by no certainly sounds like you were  fortunate to have great vision for the  organization in terms of digital goals  but then also strong leadership right  both your executive level and your  director level that are able to execute  on the plans so Aaron how do you  communicate the value of api's to  business leadership and to product  stakeholders yeah so you know I've  definitely talked about this throughout  the presentation and a little bit  earlier but I think the most important  part of this is just speaking the right  language so you know if you're talking  to a marketing person you're talking  about things in terms of customer  acquisition and you bring to the table  an actual example with data that shows  how you've been able to bring customers  into becoming customers that have you  know through the API program come in and  driven broader value for the company and  if you're talking to a P&amp;L owner or  merchant you're talking about things in  terms of EBIT and basket size right and  how you're driving value for them and  when you're talking to a someone on the  product team you're talking about things  like you know rapid test and learn and  outside an innovation and kind of how  these developer partners partnerships  have made our own products you know more  more effective and better neat meet the  needs of our customers okay great I  think we do touch on measurements but  I'll ask this question because I think  this is from a very practical level what  do you measure in your API program yeah  so we have some kind of standard things  that we typically measure you know like  the number of partners that we have for  a given API or the number of  transactions that are kind of standard  across all of them and then you know we  have some unique things for each that  are applicable to that API like the  number of rewards points that have been  awarded the number of coupons that have  been redeemed or the number of  prescriptions that have been refilled  which is great because I actually tying  the I guess the transactions on the API  level back to real business activities  and so I think the question is how do  you tie this back to business benefits  how do you measure developer  satisfaction yes so we have a couple  ways that we measure developer  satisfaction so we actually have like a  standard developer satisfaction survey  that is just like a customer  satisfaction survey and that goes out  once a year and we evaluate the results  from that to help build a better program  but beyond that I think again we have  this really strong developer evangelist  and he's out there you know every day  having meetings with some of our key  developers and you know we I think we  have a really good sun sand and our  finger on the pulse of what our  developers want because of those  frequent interactions and almost any  significant developer partner in our  ecosystem you know we're constantly  asking them what we can be doing better  you know that's such an important  function I don't see organizations  consider enough they sort of leave it as  an afterthought almost like who's gonna  wake up every day and think about how do  I make it easier for developers to  engage with us and never dress it so  it's great that you have that person in  place so Aaron how do you stay close to  developers to ensure an outside-in  perspective and ensure that your api  product design is a strong fit for their  needs yeah so I think I mentioned this I  mean we just have such a close  partnership on an ongoing basis with our  developers and I think not just  listening to you know what they're  asking for from us but sort of sharing  back to them the things that we're  thinking of and it's really a dialogue  and so you know oftentimes we bring  certain partners and very very early  into the process and you know we've had  launch partners come in even when a when  an API is in beta and I think we just  together you know we we learn so much  from each other and I think that kind of  helps us understand the developer needs  before we get too far down the pipeline  and that you know it is meeting their  needs and and then also meeting our  needs and do incentivize developers yes  we do  so we have a few ways that we do that I  think most notably we actually for a  couple of our api's we have a revenue  share so there's an actual you know  monetary compensation and I think  there's other ways of incentivizing you  know again the Walgreens brand is very  valuable so being able to talk about a  partnership with the Walgreens  and is something that is an incentive  and then finally those sort of indirect  incentives where we're enabling a  business metric that that partner has  through the services that they can get  through our API so that's awesome so  actually I think that's probably all the  time we have for questions so we will  make sure to answer our questions  you know separately online soon you see  responses there and we've got Googlers  as I said that are also answering  questions so I think that kind of wraps  us up I like to thank you once again  everyone for joining into into our  webinar today and thank you very much to  Aaron for joining us and presenting your  you know really amazing a Walgreens API  product story and reminder that we have  another webcast again next Tuesday and  we hope you can join us again then thank  you

